\details{
\code{a5_cell_to_spatial_parent()} returns, for each cell, the cell at a coarser
resolution that contains its centre. \code{a5_cell_to_spatial_children()}
returns, for each cell, the cells at a finer resolution whose centres lie
inside it. These are the location-based counterparts of
\code{\link[=a5_cell_to_parent]{a5_cell_to_parent()}} and \code{\link[=a5_cell_to_children]{a5_cell_to_children()}}, which follow the index
and often cross into neighbouring cells (see \link{a5_hierarchy}).

The two functions define an exact partition. Every fine cell has exactly
one spatial parent, and the spatial children of a coarse cell are exactly
the fine cells whose spatial parent it is, so the spatial children of cells
that tile an area tile it too, with no cell counted twice. A centre on a
shared edge goes to the cell \code{\link[=a5_lonlat_to_cell]{a5_lonlat_to_cell()}} assigns it to.
}
\section{Spatial parent}{

The result equals \code{a5_lonlat_to_cell(a5_cell_to_lonlat(cell), resolution)},
computed in one pass without converting the centre to degrees. It costs
about as much as \code{\link[=a5_lonlat_to_cell]{a5_lonlat_to_cell()}}.
}

\section{Spatial children}{

Counts vary from cell to cell and average \code{4^d} for a resolution
difference \code{d}, or \code{5 * 4^(d - 1)} from resolution 0. Candidates are the
index descendants of the cell and of its vertex neighbours, filtered by
centre containment.

The result usually equals
\code{a5_uncompact(a5_polygon_to_cells(cell, resolution), resolution)}. That
route tests centres against a polygon with great-circle edges between the
cell's vertices, while A5 cell edges are slightly curved, so at large
resolution differences it assigns a few edge cells (about 1 in 8,000 at a
difference of 6) to a neighbour that \code{a5_cell_to_spatial_parent()} would
not. \code{a5_cell_to_spatial_children()} is also faster and returns one result
per input cell.
